WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.680
Thank you. Your next question comes from the line of Emmanuel Papadakis from Deutsche Bank.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.560
Please go ahead. Thank you for taking the question. Maybe I'll take one on Iron Alamad

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.660
and Sjogren's. Given we must be relatively late in the regulatory review process, could you perhaps

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.480
just give us an update on how that's proceeding? Is everything on track? And are you expecting a

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.980
panel? And then on commercial readiness, some sense of expectations for magnitude of initial

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.660
access, breadth and willingness to prescribe, et cetera. Could you just give us a sense in

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.840
those parameters? Should we be looking at something like classic immunology launch like

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:39.240
Cosimta?

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:49.660
Thanks, Emmanuel. So again, as far as we know, no advisory committee plan for

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.960
Iron Alamad. We've had them in cycle review meetings. And so we're continuing to provide

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:03.080
FDA all the information they're requesting. So all on track from that point for a Q3 approval.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.840
I think from a market uptake standpoint, our current expectation is given that there's no

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:15.120
approved therapy in Sjogren's, we should get relatively, you know, broad access with all the

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.260
caveats that this does take time to get the environment opened up. And then we think that

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.800
physicians, given that the drug has a clean safety profile, will err on the side of trialing

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.640
the drug in patients and ultimately seeing how patients respond. I mean, this is a very

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:37.020
heterogeneous patient population. Even the STI endpoint is covering a broad range of domains.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.820
And we do see in our own...

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.620
In our own data sets, there are patients who are super responders and patients who respond less well.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.520
And so we're, I think, going to see in the marketplace, you know, for patients who respond,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.840
they'll stay on medicine and other patients will cycle off. But I think the key thing here is we

00:01:52.840 --> 00:02:00.380
have a clean safety profile, which lowers the, I think, bar for physicians to at least give patients

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:08.380
the option, given the nature of disease. These are young, often young female patients in working age

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:14.460
who obviously want better control of their symptoms and their disease. So we're optimistic

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.580
on that front as well. We continue to guide that standalone in Sjogren's, we should reach

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.820
a multi-billion dollar potential. And then, as I mentioned in my opening comments,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:29.060
Enalimab has a number of other indications that we're also pursuing both in hematology

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.060
and in immunology.

